=== FORENSICS: journald boot 49372fc6 (prior boot, -1) ===

WHAT THIS FILE IS
  A dated, read-only post-mortem of ONE machine boot, pinned to a tracked file
  because journald keeps only about three boots: the next reboot destroys the
  evidence captured here.

WHICH BOOT
  boot id      : 49372fc6-cf96-4002-8973-e25df6342aa0   (journalctl -b -1)
  span         : 2026-11-13 14:20:56 .. 2026-11-25 02:52:15  (11 d 12 h 31 min)
  housekeeping : the machine rebooted at 2026-11-25 02:52:04 CET (01:52:04 UTC;
                 witness ledger exit, returncode=-1, signaled_by=1,
                 dirty_shutdown=true). Once three more boots accumulate, boot
                 49372fc6 falls out of the journal and is gone permanently.
  captured at  : 2026-11-25T18:12:00Z  (all times in the extracts are the journal
                 own local zone, CET/CEST: short-iso with +01:00)

HOW TO READ THIS FILE
  Section 1 is the project script own report (default 6 h window) -- that run
  IS a real measurement. Section 2 is a second, independent instrument: direct
  journalctl counts. Sections 3 and 4 are raw journal extracts, filtered.

CAVEAT (the reason section 2 exists):
  Any count in the script section below that shows 0 or an empty block may be a
  BUDGET=30 timeout rather than a true zero; the independent counts in the next
  section are the authority.
  Journal-count helper in scripts/forensics_after_reboot.sh:
      out=$(timeout -k 5 30 bash -c "$1") || out=""   then  grep -c
  A query that exceeds BUDGET=30 s yields an empty string and is reported as 0.
  A 0 from that helper therefore means UNMEASURED, not none happened.
  A second, independent fault in that helper was found while pinning this boot:
  journalctl -g matches the MESSAGE text only, and earlyoom messages never
  contain the string earlyoom (the word lives in SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER, selectable
  with -t earlyoom). So the script earlyoom counts are structurally 0. See
  DISAGREEMENT lines at the end of this file.

