SECTION 5 -- DISAGREEMENTS
  DISAGREEMENT: script summary says earlyoom kills in prior boot: 0 (and the 300 h
    run the same). Independent instrument says 9482 sending SIGTERM / 1046 sending
    SIGKILL / 42973 earlyoom log lines over the boot, and 58 earlyoom entries in a
    single two-minute window. I believe the independent counts, and the proof is
    reproducible in one command pair over one window: -g earlyoom returns exactly
    "-- No entries --" for 01:20:00..01:22:00 while -t earlyoom returns 58 lines
    for the same window. Cause: journalctl -g matches the MESSAGE field only, and
    earlyoom message bodies never contain the token earlyoom; the identifier must
    be selected with -t earlyoom (SYSLOG_IDENTIFIER). The script 0 is therefore a
    filter artefact, not evidence of an idle OOM daemon.
  DISAGREEMENT (instrument trap, recorded so nobody repeats it): journalctl prints
    "-- No entries --" to STDOUT when nothing matches, so `| wc -l` or
    `| grep -c .` reports 1 for a window with no matches, not 0. Counts 1) and 2)
    above had to be corrected for this; count 4) was inspected by eye for it.
  AGREEMENT: kernel OOM kill count is 0 on both instruments (script summary and
    the direct 00:00..02:52:15 kernel scan). Both agree the reboot was orderly:
    script reports "Reached target (Power-Off|Reboot)" yes, and the direct
    02:52:00..02:53:00 query returns exactly 1 such line. So the backend death was
    NOT a kernel OOM kill and NOT a hard power loss.

